meta {
  name: Clear device token
  type: http
  seq: 4
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/method/implementor.client_notifications.clear_device_token
  body: none
  auth: inherit
}

tests {
  test("returns 200", function () {
    expect(res.status).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("has message", function () {
    expect(res.body).to.have.property("message");
  });

  test("returns success and cleared", function () {
    expect(res.body.message.success).to.equal(true);
    expect(res.body.message.cleared).to.be.a("boolean");
  });
}

settings {
  encodeUrl: true
  timeout: 0
}

docs {
  Forgets the signed-in user's push (FCM) device token, so the server stops sending pushes to this device.

  **Endpoint:** `POST /api/method/implementor.client_notifications.clear_device_token`
  **Screen:** Sign out

  > ⚠️ **Changes data** on the shared server. Running it stops pushes to the user's device until the app registers its token again (on the next sign-in, via `get_or_update_employee_token`).

  **When to call:** on sign-out, **before** dropping the session, because it needs the user's bearer token. Then delete the FCM token on the device.

  **Note the module:** this endpoint is in `implementor.client_notifications`, not `implementor.client_api`.

  **Permission:** any signed-in user. There is no company check, so it works even for a user not linked to a client company.

  **Method:** POST only. A GET is refused by Frappe with **403** and no `error_code`.

  **Body:** none.

  **Response:** always 200 for a signed-in user, also when there was nothing to clear.

  ```json
  { "message": { "success": true, "cleared": true } }
  ```

  - `cleared`: `true` when a token was removed, `false` when the user had no token saved (or no Employee record). Either way, sign-out can continue.

  **Errors:**

  - **401** (no error_code): token expired or invalid. Sign out anyway; nothing more to do.
  - **403** (no error_code): no Authorization header sent.
}
